1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,383
Our project implements two core parts of a database system:

2
00:00:03,383 --> 00:00:06,386
disk-based storage and a disk-resident B+ tree index.

3
00:00:06,386 --> 00:00:08,504
We use them to store, query,

4
00:00:08,504 --> 00:00:10,559
and delete NBA game records.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,218
Hi, we are Group 19.

6
00:00:14,218 --> 00:00:17,715
In this video, we will first explain our storage and

7
00:00:17,715 --> 00:00:21,365
B+ tree design, then demonstrate Tasks 1, 2, and 3,

8
00:00:21,365 --> 00:00:23,924
and finally analyze the query performance.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,871
The input starts from games.txt.

10
00:00:27,871 --> 00:00:32,951
The parser converts each valid row into a fixed-size record.

11
00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:37,147
StorageManager writes those records into 4-kilobyte data blocks.

12
00:00:37,147 --> 00:00:40,107
Each stored record receives a RecordId,

13
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:44,303
containing a block ID and a slot ID.

14
00:00:44,303 --> 00:00:47,064
The B+ tree is stored separately

15
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:50,377
and maps FG_PCT_home to these RecordIds.

16
00:00:50,377 --> 00:00:55,015
The data file and index file are therefore independent.

17
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,283
In the code, the main modules are separated into storage,

18
00:01:01,283 --> 00:01:03,885
index, query, and experiment components.

19
00:01:03,885 --> 00:01:07,687
StorageManager does not know about the B+ tree.

20
00:01:07,687 --> 00:01:10,189
The QueryEngine connects them by using

21
00:01:10,189 --> 00:01:13,191
the RecordIds returned by the index.

22
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,928
Each record is packed into exactly 26 bytes.

23
00:01:18,928 --> 00:01:21,513
We store the date and numeric

24
00:01:21,513 --> 00:01:24,614
game statistics using fixed-size primitive types.

25
00:01:24,614 --> 00:01:27,715
FG_PCT_home is the B+ tree key.

26
00:01:27,715 --> 00:01:30,713
The final byte is is_deleted, which acts

27
00:01:30,713 --> 00:01:33,815
as a tombstone for logical deletion.

28
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,571
Each data block is 4,096 bytes.

29
00:01:38,571 --> 00:01:42,714
The first two bytes store the record count,

30
00:01:42,714 --> 00:01:46,286
followed by fixed-size 26-byte record slots.

31
00:01:46,286 --> 00:01:51,762
This gives 157 records per block, with 12 bytes unused.

32
00:01:51,762 --> 00:01:54,738
After removing incomplete input rows, 26,552

33
00:01:54,738 --> 00:01:57,834
records require 170 data blocks.

34
00:01:57,834 --> 00:02:00,810
A record is addressed by its

35
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:03,905
block ID and slot ID.

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,915
Here is the record structure and the block implementation.

37
00:02:09,915 --> 00:02:12,840
StorageManager appends records into the active

38
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:16,351
block and flushes it when needed.

39
00:02:16,351 --> 00:02:19,393
Now we run Task 1.

40
00:02:19,393 --> 00:02:24,401
The program reports a 26-byte record, 157 records per block,

41
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:28,380
26,552 stored records, and 170 data blocks.

42
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,298
The B+ tree is also disk-based.

43
00:02:34,298 --> 00:02:38,036
Every node occupies one 4-kilobyte index page.

44
00:02:38,036 --> 00:02:41,334
Each page has a 20-byte header.

45
00:02:41,334 --> 00:02:45,842
A leaf entry stores a 4-byte key and a 6-byte

46
00:02:45,842 --> 00:02:49,580
RecordId, so each entry uses 10 bytes.

47
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:53,846
This gives a maximum leaf capacity of 407 entries,

48
00:02:53,846 --> 00:02:56,704
which defines our parameter n.

49
00:02:56,704 --> 00:03:01,761
The final heap-built tree contains 97 nodes across two levels.

50
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,973
The tree is built using iterative insertion.

51
00:03:06,973 --> 00:03:11,763
When a leaf overflows, it is split and the new

52
00:03:11,763 --> 00:03:15,268
child is inserted into its parent.

53
00:03:15,268 --> 00:03:18,306
The process can propagate upward.

54
00:03:18,306 --> 00:03:23,307
Running Task 2 gives n equals 407, 97 active nodes,

55
00:03:23,307 --> 00:03:28,215
two levels, and 95 separator keys in the root.

56
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,454
FG_PCT_home contains many duplicate values.

57
00:03:33,454 --> 00:03:37,040
In fact, 848 records have the value 0.500.

58
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,004
So a key alone cannot uniquely identify a record.

59
00:03:41,004 --> 00:03:43,363
We order leaf entries by the

60
00:03:43,363 --> 00:03:46,194
composite pair of key and RecordId.

61
00:03:46,194 --> 00:03:50,064
This lets multiple equal keys coexist while deletion can still

62
00:03:50,064 --> 00:03:52,140
identify one exact record.

63
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,747
For Task 3, we search for records

64
00:03:56,747 --> 00:03:59,590
with FG_PCT_home strictly greater than 0.5.

65
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:01,958
The tree first descends from the

66
00:04:01,958 --> 00:04:04,421
root to a boundary leaf.

67
00:04:04,421 --> 00:04:07,718
Because duplicate 0.5 values can span several leaves,

68
00:04:07,718 --> 00:04:11,318
the implementation may walk left through previous pointers.

69
00:04:11,318 --> 00:04:13,687
It then scans forward through the

70
00:04:13,687 --> 00:04:16,529
linked leaves and collects matching RecordIds.

71
00:04:16,529 --> 00:04:20,413
The query returns 6,054 records, or 22.8 percent of the

72
00:04:20,413 --> 00:04:23,634
dataset, with mean FG_PCT_home equal to 0.536966.

73
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,315
These are the three important functions.

74
00:04:30,315 --> 00:04:37,190
range_greater_than defines the range, leftmost_leaf_for_key handles duplicate boundary values,

75
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:41,619
and collect_range follows the leaf chain

76
00:04:41,619 --> 00:04:46,226
to collect all matching entries.

77
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,988
The first retrieval strategy is naïve.

78
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:54,055
After the B+ tree returns matching RecordIds,

79
00:04:54,055 --> 00:04:56,644
we fetch every record independently.

80
00:04:56,644 --> 00:05:00,509
If several RecordIds belong to the same data block,

81
00:05:00,509 --> 00:05:03,496
that block can be requested repeatedly.

82
00:05:03,496 --> 00:05:07,580
For the official predicate, this produces 6,052 application-level data-block read

83
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:11,364
calls and a median runtime of 4.966 milliseconds.

84
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,339
The grouped strategy keeps exactly the same B+ tree result.

85
00:05:17,339 --> 00:05:21,678
The only change is how the returned RecordIds are fetched.

86
00:05:21,678 --> 00:05:24,205
We group them by block ID,

87
00:05:24,205 --> 00:05:28,073
read each required block once, and then access all required

88
00:05:28,073 --> 00:05:30,148
slots from that block.

89
00:05:30,148 --> 00:05:33,430
Application-level data reads fall from 6,052 to 169,

90
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:35,505
a 97.21 percent reduction.

91
00:05:35,505 --> 00:05:38,788
Median runtime improves from 4.966 to 0.902 milliseconds,

92
00:05:38,788 --> 00:05:41,240
giving a 5.5 times speedup.

93
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,215
Both versions use the same B+ tree,

94
00:05:46,215 --> 00:05:51,016
the same heap data layout, and return the same records.

95
00:05:51,016 --> 00:05:55,818
The naïve version calls record retrieval once for every RID.

96
00:05:55,818 --> 00:06:00,097
The grouped version first groups the RIDs by block and

97
00:06:00,097 --> 00:06:02,394
then performs block-oriented retrieval.

98
00:06:02,394 --> 00:06:06,360
So this experiment isolates only the retrieval strategy.

99
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,914
However, grouping alone does not make the heap-indexed query faster

100
00:06:11,914 --> 00:06:14,014
than a linear scan.

101
00:06:14,014 --> 00:06:16,782
The matching records are scattered across every

102
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:19,645
one of the 170 data blocks.

103
00:06:19,645 --> 00:06:23,559
Grouped B+ retrieval therefore still touches all 170 blocks and

104
00:06:23,559 --> 00:06:25,945
takes 0.902 milliseconds, while the sequential

105
00:06:25,945 --> 00:06:28,427
linear scan takes 0.547 milliseconds.

106
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,819
We therefore ran an additional experiment on physical locality.

107
00:06:33,819 --> 00:06:36,620
Instead of storing records in input order,

108
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:38,894
we stably sort them by FG_PCT_home

109
00:06:38,894 --> 00:06:41,258
before writing the data file.

110
00:06:41,258 --> 00:06:43,895
The B+ tree remains a separate secondary

111
00:06:43,895 --> 00:06:46,623
index containing key and RecordId pairs.

112
00:06:46,623 --> 00:06:50,352
For the same query, qualifying records now occupy only 40

113
00:06:50,352 --> 00:06:52,716
data blocks instead of 170.

114
00:06:52,716 --> 00:06:56,244
Median grouped retrieval falls from 0.902 to 0.432 milliseconds,

115
00:06:56,244 --> 00:06:58,245
a 2.09 times speedup.

116
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,439
These four configurations separate the main costs.

117
00:07:04,439 --> 00:07:07,833
Linear scan takes 0.547 milliseconds.

118
00:07:07,833 --> 00:07:11,096
Naïve B+ retrieval takes 4.966 milliseconds

119
00:07:11,096 --> 00:07:14,490
because of repeated data-block accesses.

120
00:07:14,490 --> 00:07:18,407
Grouping reduces it to 0.902 milliseconds.

121
00:07:18,407 --> 00:07:23,890
Finally, grouped retrieval over FG_PCT_home-clustered storage takes 0.432 milliseconds.

122
00:07:23,890 --> 00:07:27,153
The corresponding application read counts are

123
00:07:27,153 --> 00:07:30,547
170, 6,052, 169, and 39.

124
00:07:30,547 --> 00:07:34,986
These are application-level reads, not physical-device reads.

125
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,991
The result also depends on query selectivity.

126
00:07:37,991 --> 00:07:41,598
At high selectivity, many records match and the heap index

127
00:07:41,598 --> 00:07:45,645
still touches most data blocks, so sequential scanning is efficient.

128
00:07:45,645 --> 00:07:48,988
As selectivity decreases, indexed retrieval becomes more attractive.

129
00:07:48,988 --> 00:07:51,188
At 4.91 percent selectivity, heap grouped

130
00:07:51,188 --> 00:07:53,827
retrieval slightly beats the linear scan.

131
00:07:53,827 --> 00:07:56,818
At 0.99 percent, it is clearly faster.

132
00:07:56,818 --> 00:08:00,513
Clustering improves locality further and shifts this crossover earlier.

133
00:08:00,513 --> 00:08:02,713
These crossover points are specific to

134
00:08:02,713 --> 00:08:05,000
this dataset and benchmark environment.

135
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,656
After retrieval, Task 3 deletes all 6,054 matching records.

136
00:08:08,656 --> 00:08:10,832
In the B+ tree, deletion uses

137
00:08:10,832 --> 00:08:13,443
the exact key and RecordId pair.

138
00:08:13,443 --> 00:08:16,821
An underfilled node first attempts redistribution from a sibling;

139
00:08:16,821 --> 00:08:20,825
otherwise nodes are merged and the parent is repaired recursively.

140
00:08:20,825 --> 00:08:23,506
In the data file, deletion is logical:

141
00:08:23,506 --> 00:08:26,117
is_deleted changes from zero to one.

142
00:08:26,117 --> 00:08:29,773
Records are not moved, so existing RecordIds remain stable.

143
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,752
After deleting all values strictly above 0.5,

144
00:08:32,752 --> 00:08:36,504
the tree shrinks from 97 to 76 active nodes.

145
00:08:36,504 --> 00:08:39,971
The number of leaves falls from 96 to 75,

146
00:08:39,971 --> 00:08:42,651
while the height remains two levels.

147
00:08:42,651 --> 00:08:45,689
The root now contains 74 separator keys.

148
00:08:45,689 --> 00:08:48,441
The largest remaining key is exactly 0.500,

149
00:08:48,441 --> 00:08:50,674
which is correct because the predicate

150
00:08:50,674 --> 00:08:52,997
is strictly greater than 0.5.

151
00:08:52,997 --> 00:08:55,320
Structural validation passes after deletion.

152
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,579
For Task 3, we use grouped

153
00:08:59,579 --> 00:09:02,675
retrieval on the heap-organized data file.

154
00:09:02,675 --> 00:09:05,254
Before deletion, the B-plus-tree query finds

155
00:09:05,254 --> 00:09:08,349
6,054 records with FG_PCT_home above 0.5.

156
00:09:08,349 --> 00:09:12,580
A linear scan returns the same count and average of

157
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:14,849
0.536966, confirming the result.

158
00:09:14,849 --> 00:09:17,945
We then delete all matching records.

159
00:09:17,945 --> 00:09:22,278
When both queries are repeated, each returns zero matches.

160
00:09:22,278 --> 00:09:26,694
The final output confirms 76 active B-plus-tree nodes, two levels,

161
00:09:26,694 --> 00:09:28,964
and successful structural validation.

162
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,310
The B+ tree narrows the search,

163
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:35,241
but it does not determine query performance.

164
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:39,205
Grouping RecordIds cuts redundant reads, while clustering improves data locality.

165
00:09:39,205 --> 00:09:42,136
Selectivity determines how many records we fetch.

166
00:09:42,136 --> 00:09:45,135
Grouping reduced read calls from 6,052 to 169,

167
00:09:45,135 --> 00:09:49,100
and clustering reduced unique data blocks from 170 to 40.

168
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:52,634
Together, these results show that storage layout and retrieval strategy

169
00:09:52,634 --> 00:09:55,220
matter as much as index traversal.
